<onlyinclude>'''Parallel computing''' means splitting a computational job so that several processors or cores can work on parts of it at the same time. It can finish work sooner, but only when the parts can cooperate without spending too much time waiting or exchanging information.

== How it works ==
Imagine processing a million independent photographs. Several workers can each process a different group. But when one worker needs another's results before continuing, coordination can slow the job.

A multicore computer can run threads that share memory. A cluster can run processes on different machines and send messages between them. GPUs can execute many related operations over large datasets.

== What it does not guarantee ==
Doubling processors rarely halves elapsed time: some work must remain sequential, while communication and coordination take time. Parallel computing also requires careful handling of shared data to avoid incorrect results.

== Further reading ==
* [https://www.openmp.org/spec-html/5.2/openmp.html OpenMP 5.2 specification].
* [https://www.mpi-forum.org/docs/ MPI standards].

== Distinctions ==
Data parallelism performs related work on different pieces of data; task parallelism runs different activities. Concurrency means tasks can make progress independently, but does not necessarily mean they execute simultaneously. Parallel computing is not the same as scaling, which describes how a system changes as resources or workload change. Speedup can also increase energy use or cost.

